systemd service file configuration (usually found in /etc/systemd/system/).
systemd service file, used to manage background processes (daemons) in Linux.


.service file is a plain-text configuration file that tells systemd how to manage, monitor, and run a specific background process or application (a daemon). It acts as a declarative script, replacing older, complex SysV init shell scripts with clean, predictable blocks of settings.


📂 Where .service Files Live

Systemd looks for service files in three main directories, depending on who created them:
• /lib/systemd/system/ (or /usr/lib/...): Package-managed services. When you install a program like Nginx or Docker via apt or dnf, its default service file goes here. Do not edit files here directly.
• /etc/systemd/system/: Custom or overridden services. This is where you place your own custom service files or system administrator overrides. Files here have the highest priority and will override anything in /lib/.
• /run/systemd/system/: Runtime services. Dynamic, temporary services generated on the fly at runtime (wiped out on reboot).


when you use systemd, it manages your program(execstart in .service file) so it runs as a daemon (background service) in the system. However, how systemd tracks and monitors that daemon depends entirely on the Type= setting.

When systemd treats a process as the "main process" (which happens by default when Type=simple), it means systemd keeps a direct, unblinking eye on the exact program it launched.


How it runs as the "Main Process" (Type=simple)

In a standard systemd service, the process runs as the main process through a straightforward chain of events:

[systemd (PID 1)] 
       │
       ▼ 
[Your Program (e.g., Python script/Binary)] <─── systemd watches THIS PID directly as the "Main Process"

1. Direct Launch: When you run systemctl start yourservice, systemd directly spawns the program defined in ExecStart=.
2. Immediate Ownership: Systemd looks at the Process ID (PID) of that newly launched program and notes it down: "This specific PID is the Main Process."
3. Continuous Monitoring: Because it is the main process, systemd ties the lifetime of the service directly to it.
	• If your program is running, the service status is active (running).
	• If your program exits or crashes, systemd instantly knows it, changes the status to inactive or failed, and triggers a restart if configured to do so.
In this mode(type=simple), your program must not try to put itself into the background (it shouldn't daemonize or clone itself). It should just run normally, and let systemd handle keeping it in the background of the system.

-----------------------

If your program is traditional software (like Apache or Nginx) that is coded to automatically clone itself into the background when it starts, a default systemd setup breaks. Here is what happens if you don't use Type=forking for those programs:
1. Systemd launches the program (ExecStart).
2. The program immediately forks itself to become a background daemon and closes its original starter process.
3. Systemd sees the original starter process close and thinks: "Oh, the main process just exited! The service must be finished or broken."
4. Systemd will then aggressively kill off the child process (the actual daemon you wanted) because it thinks the service is stopping.
Setting Type=forking tells systemd: "Expect this program to clone itself. Wait for the starter process to exit, then look for the child it left behind, and treat that child as the new Main Process."

