📝 Structure of a .service File

A service file uses three essential sections: [Unit], [Service], and [Install].

[Unit]
Description=My Custom Python Web App
After=network.target
Requires=postgresql.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User=webappuser
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/myapp
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /var/www/myapp/app.py
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target


1. The [Unit] Section

This section defines metadata about the service and establishes its position in the boot order or dependency graph.

• Description=: A friendly, readable name for the service shown when running systemctl status.

•(optional)After=: Defines ordering. It tells systemd to wait until the listed resource (like network.target) is up before starting this service.

•(optional)Requires=: Defines a strict dependency. If the listed service (like postgresql.service) fails to start, your service will fail to start too.

•(optional) Wants=:A loose dependency. Systemd will attempt to start the listed service alongside yours, but won't crash your service if that one fails.




2. The [Service] Section

This section configures the core execution behavior—how to run, track, and secure the process.

• Type=: Configures the startup notification style.
	• simple (default): Assumes the process starts immediately and stays in the foreground.

	• forking: Used for traditional daemons that spin off a background child process and exit the parent process.
       oneshot: the script performs a single task and then exits, rather than running continuously in the background like a web server
• User= & Group=: The Linux user account that should execute the app, following the principle of least privilege.

• ExecStart=: The exact absolute path to the command or binary that launches your program.
------------
*) ExecStart command 

specifies the exact command and arguments to execute when starting a service
It tells systemd exactly which binary, script, or program to run to get the application up and running.

ExecStart sits under the [Service] block:

Key Characteristics of ExecStart

• Requires Full Paths: systemd does not read your user shell profile or $PATH variables. You must always provide the absolute path to the executable (e.g., /usr/bin/python3 instead of just python3).

• Defines the Main Process: Unless configured as a background/forking process (Type=forking), the process launched by ExecStart is treated by the system as the main process of that service.

• Strict Constraints: For standard services, exactly one ExecStart directive is allowed. The only exception is when Type=oneshot is specified, which allows you to list multiple ExecStart lines that run sequentially.

you can put a command or multiple commands or script file to execute when systemd starts this .service file
