install section in depth :

The [Install] section does absolutely nothing when a service is actively running.

[Install] is purely a set of instructions used by the systemctl enable command to figure out how to hook this service into the Linux boot process so that it starts automatically when the computer turns on.

🗺️ The Concept: Linux Booting is a Chain Reaction
When your Linux computer boots up, systemd doesn't just start everything at once in a chaotic scramble. It follows a structured dependency tree made of Targets.
Think of a "Target" as a milestone or a room in a house:
1. First, the system hits local-fs.target (hard drives are mounted).
2. Then, it hits network.target (the internet/network card is ready).
3. Finally, it reaches multi-user.target (the system is fully booted, online, and ready for multiple users to log in).

The [Install] section is how your service file says: "Hey systemd, when you reach milestone X during boot, please make sure you pull me into the sequence and start me up too."


🔍 How it Works Under the Hood (Symbolic Links)

When you type: sudo systemctl enable myapp.service, 

systemd reads the [Install] section of your file.
If your file looks like this:

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Systemd translates WantedBy=multi-user.target into a literal file system command. It goes to the folder for that milestone (/etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/) and creates a symbolic link (a shortcut) pointing back to your service file.

📁 /etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/
   └── 🔗 myapp.service  ---> (Points back to your actual service file)

Now, the next time the computer boots up and systemd processes the multi-user.target milestone, it looks inside that .wants/ folder, sees the shortcut to your service, and launches it.
If you run sudo systemctl disable myapp.service, systemd simply deletes that shortcut. The service file remains intact, but it will no longer start at boot.

🛠️ Common Directives Inside [Install]

• WantedBy=multi-user.target: The most common. It means "start my service when the system is fully booted into normal terminal mode."
• WantedBy=graphical.target: Means "start my service only after the graphical desktop interface (like GNOME or KDE) has successfully loaded."
• WantedBy=timers.target: Used inside .timer files to ensure the system timer engine starts tracking your scheduled events at boot.
