.timer

 you create two separate files in /etc/systemd/system/ with matching names (e.g., backup.timer and backup.service).

 1. The Service File (/etc/systemd/system/backup.service)

 This file only defines what to run. Because it relies on the timer, it does not need an [Install] section.

 [Unit]
Description=Run My Custom Backup Script

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/my-backup-script.sh

(Note: Type=oneshot is perfect here because the script performs a single task and then exits, rather than running continuously in the background like a web server).


2. The Timer File (/etc/systemd/system/backup.timer)
This file defines when to run. By default, systemd looks for a service file with the exact same prefix name (e.g., backup.timer triggers backup.service).

[Unit]
Description=Trigger the Backup Script every hour

[Timer]
# Option A: Monotonic (relative time)
OnBootSec=15min
OnUnitActiveSec=1h

# Option B: 
# Real-time/Calendar (like a traditional cron job)
# OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target


⏱️ Key Timer Settings to Know

Inside the [Timer] section, you have two primary ways to schedule your service:
• Monotonic Timers (Relative Time): These trigger relative to system events.
	• OnBootSec=: How long after the machine boots up to trigger the service for the first time.
	• OnUnitActiveSec=: How long to wait after the service was last activated. This creates a continuous loop (e.g., run every 1 hour).
• Real-time Timers (Calendar Time): These function like traditional Linux cron jobs using the OnCalendar= directive.
	• OnCalendar=daily: Runs once a day at midnight.
	• OnCalendar=Mon,Tue *-*-* 18:30:00: Runs at 6:30 PM every Monday and Tuesday.


⚙️ How to Activate It

When using this pattern, you never enable the service. Instead, you enable and start the timer unit.

(note: when you enable something (.service , .timer) using systemctl, the systemd reach out to .target file mentioned inside [install] sectioncreate a link (a shortcut) (/etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/) pointing back to your configuration file(.service, .timer) file based)

# Reload systemd to recognize the new files
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Enable and start the TIMER (not the service)
sudo systemctl enable --now backup.timer

# Check the status of your timer
sudo systemctl status backup.timer

# View all active timers and see exactly when they will run next
systemctl list-timers

