Logging and troubleshooting in systemd service configuration

In systemd, logging and troubleshooting are deeply integrated. Systemd captures everything a service sends to standard output (stdout) and standard error (stderr) automatically, routing it to a centralized logging system called the journal.

Because of this unified design, you don't have to hunt down scattered log files in /var/log/ to find out why a service failed.


🪵 1. The Core Tool: journalctl

journalctl is the command-line utility used to query and view systemd logs. To troubleshoot a specific service, you will heavily rely on filtering options.

Essential Troubleshooting Filters

View live logs (Follow mode ):
sudo journalctl -u myapp.service -f

Show logs since the last boot:
sudo journalctl -u myapp.service -b

Show logs within a specific time window:
sudo journalctl -u myapp.service --since "1 hour ago"
sudo journalctl -u myapp.service --since "2026-11-05 12:00:00" --until "2026-11-05 13:00:00"


🚨 2. The 3-Step Troubleshooting Workflow

When a systemd service enters a failed state, follow this standard sequence to diagnose it:

Step 1: Check the Status Summary

Run systemctl status myapp.service. This gives you an immediate bird's-eye view.
• Look at the Active: line. If it says failed (Result: exit-code), systemd tried to run it, but the application crashed.
• Look at the Main PID. This tells you the active process ID.
• Read the last 10 log lines printed at the bottom of this status screen. They often contain the direct error message (e.g., "Permission denied" or "Port already in use").

Step 2: Read the Full Journal Context

If the 10 lines at the bottom of the status screen aren't enough, open the complete log history for that unit:

sudo journalctl -u myapp.service -n 50 --no-pager


Step 3: Validate File Syntax
If your service refuses to load at all when you run systemctl daemon-reload, you likely made a typo in the .service file. Check it using the linting tool:

systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service

This command checks for misspellings, missing absolute paths, or broken structural logic inside your configuration file before you try to start it.


💡 Common Service Failures & Quick Fixes

1. "Executable path is not absolute"
	• The Cause: You wrote ExecStart=python3 app.py.
	• The Fix: Systemd does not search your user $PATH. You must use full absolute paths: ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /var/www/app.py.
2. "Permission Denied" / Code 217/USER
	• The Cause: You specified User=webappuser, but that user doesn't have read/execute permissions for the directory containing the application binary or script.
	• The Fix: Check directory permissions with ls -la or verify the user actually exists on the system.
3. Process exits immediately / Code 0/SUCCESS
	• The Cause: Your service file uses Type=simple (default), but the script you ran backgrounded itself (forked). Systemd thinks the application finished its job and shuts down the unit tracking.
	• The Fix: Change the configuration to Type=forking or configure your application binary to run strictly in the foreground.

