Passing credentials or secrets to script or command inside .service configuration file

The most secure way to pass sensitive information like passwords or API keys to a background daemon service is by using environment variables or dedicated secret management tools, rather than hardcoding them or passing them as command-line arguments.

Because background daemons run continuously and often start automatically at system boot, how you inject these secrets depends on your operating system and deployment environment.


1. Environment Variables via Init Systems (Recommended for Linux)

If your daemon(.service) is managed by a system init system like systemd, you should inject secrets as environment variables using secure configuration files.
• How it works: You define the variables in a file that is heavily restricted using file permissions (chmod 600 or 400), ensuring only root or the specific daemon user can read it.
• Systemd Example:
	1. Create a file at /etc/daemon-secrets.env

	API_KEY=your_actual_api_key_here
	DB_PASSWORD=your_secure_password

2. Restrict access so only the root/service owner can read it: chown root:root /etc/daemon-secrets.env && chmod 600 /etc/daemon-secrets.env

3. In your systemd service file (/etc/systemd/system/mydaemon.service), use the EnvironmentFile directive:

[Service]
User=daemonuser
EnvironmentFile=/etc/daemon-secrets.env
ExecStart=/usr/bin/mydaemonexecutable

